Termination of tenancy

ACT XX OF 1950
ACT XX OF 1950
SIND TENANCY ACT, 1950
CHAPTER II
PERMANENT TENANTS
13. Termination. of `tenancy.-Notwithstanding any agreement,
usage, decree or order of a Court of Law, the rights conferred on a permanent
tenant by the Act shall not be terminated unless the land in respect of which
tenancy rights are held is acquired or requisitioned by Government for a public
purpose or unless such tenant-
(a) voluntarily surrenders the tenancy,-
(b) abandons the tenancy;
(c) has used such land for a purpose other than agriculture
without the written permission of the landlord;
(d) has mortgaged, charged, alienated, assigned leased or sub-let
the land ;
(e) fails to cultivate the land personally ;
(f) fails,, without sufficient cause, to cultivate the land in the
manner, or to the extent, customary in the locality in which the land. is
situated ;
(g) has done any act which is destructive or permanently injurious
to the land ;
(h) fails to pay the due share of produce to the landlord in cash
or kind, as the case may be ;
(i) is after the commencement of this Act convicted by a Court of
law of the following offences, namely ;-
(i) cattle theft or receiving stolen cattle ;
(ii) abduction ;
(iii) robbery or dacoity ;
(iv) theft of the crop of his landlord ;
(v) criminal misappropriation or criminal breach of trust, or
cheating in respect of the property of his landlord.
On termination of permanent rights for any of the above reasons
the landlord may remove him without reference to the tribunal ;
Provided that in case of a conviction for the offence of abduction
or robbery or dacoity, the tenancy right shall devolve as if the permanent
tenant were dead.
